  "worked_solution": "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)})}^{-2t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {2}^{-2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}{e}^{5} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}e \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =e[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+4[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}e \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{2}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
